{"id":"u-09770c59-38cc-4fce-a347-3e7fcb9f2ac7","src":"community","title":"ReBuilding Strength","author":"Tiziano S.","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/WbfI1l-rebuilding-strength","desc":"This program is designed for mid-late intermediates and its purpose is to build strength while maintaining and developing muscle mass through structured progressive overload. The main lifts use a double progression system, beginning with moderate loads and gradually increasing intensity while allowing performance to dictate when reps and weight progru iess.\nThe first six-week block establishes a base of volume and technical consistency before a deload. The second six-week block builds on that foundation with progressively heavier work and a more demanding top-set structure, followed by a final deload and four-day testing phase.\nAccessory movements use an 8–12 rep hypertrophy range to provide targeted muscle growth without interfering with progression on the primary lifts. The program assumes the ability to increase of one rep or minimum weight increment session-to-session, the goal is to do at least one more rep on at least one set session-to-session.\nAccessory lifts are not expected to match the strength of their primary movement. For programming and testing purposes, use the following rough conversion from the primary lift 1RM:\n Front squat: ~85% of back squat 1RM\n Close grip bench press: ~92% of regular bench 1RM\nIncline bench press: ~80–85% of regular bench press  1RM\n Push press: ~105–110% of regular OHP 1RM\nThese are starting estimates, not hard limits. Individual differences in technique, proportions, and training history can easily move the actual number by several percentage points. When a variation has an established training max, use the actual number instead of the conversion.\nFor hypertrophy movements, no 1RM is required. Select a load that allows progression through the prescribed 8–12 rep range while maintaining good technique and proximity to failure.\nWarm-up protocol\nThe purpose of the warm-up is to prepare the movement without accumulating meaningful fatigue. Warm-up sets should become progressively heavier while reps decrease.\nBack squat: use 3–4 warm-up sets before the top set.\nA useful template is:\n~45–50% × 5–6 → ~60–65% × 4 → ~75–80% × 2\nThe final warm-up should feel like a preparation set, not a fifth work set.\nDeadlift: use 3 warm-up sets.\nA useful template is:\n~60% × 4 → ~75% × 3 → ~85–90% × 2\nBench press, incline bench, close grip bench, front squat, OHP and push press: use 2 progressive warm-up sets, adjusting the absolute loads to the movement.\nFor these, start around roughly 55–70% of the day's working weight. With lower body movements starting at a lower percentage.\nHypertrophy movements do not require any warm up.\nInitial mobility work at user's discretion.\nAs a general rule, the final warm-up should be approximately 80–90% of the working weight for 1–2 reps.\nThe ultimate  goal is to accumulate high-quality training, progressively increase performance, recover sufficiently, and arrive at the testing phase stronger than when the program began.","level":["Intermediate"],"goals":["Muscle","Strength"],"equipment":["Garage Gym"],"minutes":40,"joins":1,"rate":0,"rateCount":1,"updated":"2026-10-20T12:54:14.501Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Day 1","Day 2","Day 3","Day 4","Day 5"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"ss":[{"n":"Squat (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":4,"pct":77.5},{"r":6,"pct":75}]},{"n":"Pull-Up (Neutral Grip, Weighted)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":9},{"r":8,"rpe":8}]}]},{"ss":[{"n":"Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":4,"pct":77.5},{"r":6,"pct":75}]},{"n":"Lateral Raise (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":9.5},{"r":8,"rpe":10}]}]}]},{"ex":[{"ss":[{"n":"Sumo Deadlift (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":4,"pct":77.5,"x":2}]},{"n":"Incline Curl (Dumbbell)","sets":[{"r":8,"rpe":9},{"r":8,"rpe":10}]}]},{"ss":[{"n":"Push Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"r":4,"pct":77.5},{"r":6,"pct":75}]},{"n":"Single Leg Calf Raise (Weig